---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Builder from '../../components/Builder.astro';
---
<Base title="Privacy" description="What Clash of Claudes does on your Mac: agent output never leaves your machine, nothing else does unless you turn a team board on, your agent configuration is never edited, and status stays on localhost.">
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Clash of Claudes', item: 'https://clashofclaudes.crevn.xyz/' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Privacy', item: 'https://clashofclaudes.crevn.xyz/privacy/' },
    ],
  })} />
  <section class="page-hero" aria-labelledby="page-title">
    <h1 class="title" id="page-title" data-split="lines" data-intro>What stays on your Mac</h1>
    <p class="page-hero__lede">Short version: all of it, unless you turn on a team board. Here is exactly what the app touches, what it sends, and what it never does.</p>
    <div class="page-hero__art drop-in" aria-hidden="true">
      <img src="/img/tent-codex.webp" alt="" width="360" height="330" fetchpriority="high" />
      <Builder sheet="craftsman-codex" pose="cheer" />
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="doc">
    <nav class="doc__toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p>On this page</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#network">Network</a></li>
        <li><a href="#team">With a team</a></li>
        <li><a href="#config">Your agent configuration</a></li>
        <li><a href="#status">How status works</a></li>
        <li><a href="#notifications">Notifications</a></li>
        <li><a href="#site">This website</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>
    <div class="doc__body">
      <section id="network" aria-labelledby="network-h">
        <h2 id="network-h">Network</h2>
        <p>Agent output never leaves your machine. Prompts, file names and folders do not either, unless you turn on the team board below. The app itself makes no network connections except these:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>The update check.</strong> One request to see whether a newer beta exists. Turn it off in Settings.</li>
          <li><strong>Reports you choose to send</strong> from Help › Report a Bug…, Help › Request a Feature… or Settings › Beta feedback. A report holds what you type, the app, macOS and agent versions, counts of workspaces and sessions, and the last error the app showed. Never workspace names or tasks. A screenshot is included only if you tick it, and you see everything before sending.</li>
          <li><strong>Anonymous usage counts, only if you turn them on</strong> in the welcome or Settings › Data &amp; privacy. They are off until you do. Each count is one of a fixed few: the app opened (with how many workspaces and builders you have), a session started (and which agent), a terminal started, a permission prompt allowed or denied, the Kanban opened. Each carries the app, macOS and chip, and a random id made when you turn sharing on and deleted when you turn it off. Never names, paths, tasks, prompts or output. They go to PostHog’s EU cloud with no profile of you. Turn it off and they stop at once.</li>
          <li><strong>Your Vercel team’s board, only if you turn it on</strong> in Settings › Team. It is off until you do. <a href="#team">With a team</a>, below, says what it sends and who can read it.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Your agents still talk to their own services the way they always do, with your own logins. The app does not sit in between.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="team" aria-labelledby="team-h">
        <h2 id="team-h">With a team</h2>
        <p>Everything here is off until you turn it on in Settings › Team, and it goes through the <code>vercel</code> command and login you already have. The app never sees your Vercel token.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>What goes to the board.</strong> For each workspace you choose to share: the state of its sessions (working, needs you, finished, stuck, paused, closed) and a map of your village, which is the names of your shared workspaces and builders and where they stand. With Share details on, which is the default, also the task, the tool call a builder asks about, its questions, the agent’s last reply and the names of changed files. Turn Share details off and only the states and the map go. Terminal output and file contents never go.</li>
          <li><strong>Where it is kept.</strong> On Vercel Board, in the United States, under your Vercel team.</li>
          <li><strong>Who can read it.</strong> A village is closed to everyone you did not invite, and Settings always lists who can see yours, read from the board itself. Only an owner of the Vercel team can close a village: if you are a member, everyone on the team can see yours until an owner does, and Settings says so.</li>
          <li><strong>People you invite.</strong> An invite writes a teammate’s Vercel email and level to the board’s access rules. Nobody can act in your village until you pair their Mac, by a code you send them and a number you both check. Paired Macs and phones are kept in your macOS Keychain. Your Mac checks every action against that list, whatever the board allows, and each action is written to the builder’s thread.</li>
          <li><strong>Lending a builder.</strong> A teammate you paired can hand a builder you lend a task. It runs on your Mac, with your logins and your elixir, in its own copy of the repository cut from what <code>origin</code> has. When it finishes, the app pushes that one new branch to <code>origin</code> with your git credentials: never forced, never over an existing branch. The task and whether it was taken are written to the board. Lend only to people you would give that access.</li>
          <li><strong>The phone remote.</strong> A small web app on your own Vercel team, as the project <code>village-remote</code>. It reads the same board and adds each paired phone’s name and public key, the address its notifications go to, and every action a phone sends. Notifications pass through Apple’s, Google’s or Mozilla’s push service and carry the builder’s name, the workspace and, with Share details on, the command or question. Whoever can deploy to that project on your team can make it act as your phone and read every village that lets the remote in; your Mac pauses the remote when it sees a deployment it did not make.</li>
          <li><strong>Your own agents.</strong> They run with your Vercel login, so one could change your village’s access rules. Settings checks the rules when it opens and offers to put them back.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
      <section id="config" aria-labelledby="config-h">
        <h2 id="config-h">Your agent configuration</h2>
        <p>The app never edits your agents’ own configuration: nothing under <code>~/.claude</code>, <code>~/.codex</code>, <code>~/.gemini</code>, <code>~/.config/opencode</code> or <code>~/.grok</code>, and nothing in a project’s <code>.claude</code>, <code>.codex</code>, <code>.gemini</code>, <code>.opencode</code> or <code>.grok</code> folder.</p>
        <p>To receive status events it hands each session a private settings file kept in the app’s own data folder, <code class="path">~/Library/<wbr />Application&nbsp;Support/<wbr />dev.clashofclaudes.desktop/<wbr />sessions/</code>, and deletes it when the session closes.</p>
        <p>To resume an Antigravity session the app reads, and never writes, <code class="path">~/.gemini/<wbr />antigravity-cli/<wbr />history.jsonl</code>. A Grok Build session starts with its own id, and to resume it the app only checks that Grok saved it under <code class="path">~/.grok/<wbr />sessions/</code>.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="status" aria-labelledby="status-h">
        <h2 id="status-h">How status works</h2>
        <p>Agents report working, needs you and finished to a small server that listens only on your Mac (localhost), protected by a random token for each session. Nothing outside your machine can reach it.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="notifications" aria-labelledby="notifications-h">
        <h2 id="notifications-h">Notifications</h2>
        <p>When the app is in the background it can show a macOS notification when a session needs you or finishes. The title is the builder’s name and the body says “needs you” or “finished”. When an agent asks for permission, the notification has an Allow button that answers that one prompt. Notifications never include terminal output, and never your own task text. One exception, if you lend builders: when a teammate borrows one, the notification shows the first line of the task they wrote.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="site" aria-labelledby="site-h">
        <h2 id="site-h">This website</h2>
        <p>No cookies and no trackers. The site counts page views with Vercel Web Analytics, which sets no cookies and keeps no profile of you: it sees the page, where the visit came from, and the country, browser and device type. The download buttons ask GitHub’s public API for the latest version number and remember the answer in your browser for an hour. Those are the only requests this site makes to anyone else.</p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
